<section class="g-dark" id="why">
  <div class="wrap sv-story">
    <div>
      <div class="op-n" data-rv=""><b>22</b><span>days to close, after five months open.</span></div>
      <h2 data-rv="" style="--d:60ms">We got paid to talk a client out of a search.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sv-story-b">
      <p class="sv-story-o" data-rv="">The first time it happened, it felt like we had done something wrong.</p>
      <p data-rv="">
        A role had been open five months. <b class="sv-91" data-count="327">327</b> profiles had been through it, and the
        team was sure the talent wasn’t out there. We ran the scorecard session, then re-read the eleven people already passed
        over. Two came back into contention. One was hired: someone turned down in month two by a panellist who was, quite
        reasonably, testing for something the role didn’t need.
      </p>
      <p data-rv="">
        The role closed in twenty-two days. The work that unlocked it was two days and a fixed fee. Under the model the
        rest of this industry uses, that’s barely worth billing. It was the most useful thing we did for anyone that
        quarter.
      </p>
      <p data-rv="">
        So we built the rest of it that way on purpose. Most of what goes wrong in hiring happens before anyone meets a
        candidate, and you can’t fix that half if you only get paid for the other half.
      </p>
